00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:40.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My feeling of excitement became horror.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.740
It's true, women are choosing
not to study computer science.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.780
But that choice it's not made in a vacuum.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.080
It's a choice constrained by
the reality of the world they live in,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.790
a world that tells them from a young age,
they do not belong.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.990
Some of those signals can be overt

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.765
like the case of Barbie's laughable
computer engineering career.

00:02:05.765 --> 00:02:10.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But some of them can be more subtle,

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:14.440
like the case of Alana King,
a black female computer science major.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.770
Every time she went to
the Stanford CS Building,

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:23.440
she would get asked, hey, are you lost?

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.920
Until finally she got so
fed up with it that she wrote a blog post.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.895
No, I'm not lost.

00:02:29.895 --> 00:02:33.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What about the case of Isis Wenger.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.120
When she was featured on
a recruiting poster for

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.220
her companies software engineering
team the ad blew up with comments.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:47.240
But with people saying she must be
a hired model and not an actual engineer.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:54.310
These stereotypes surrounding us since
birth can have powerful effects.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.180
No matter how hard our conscious
mind seek to reject them.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.630
Stanford psychologist,
Claude Steele, coined the term,

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:07.070
stereotype threat, to describe a situation
in which an individual feels at risk

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:08.720
of conforming to a stereotype.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.110
In his experiment, when African-Americans
were asked to identify their race

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.180
before taking an aptitude test, they
performed more poorly than white students.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.450
However, if you remove this race question,
all of a sudden,

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:27.590
that difference, all of it disappears.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:32.020
And these findings have been
replicated many times since.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:38.170
In a test of sports ability, white men
perform more poorly than black men.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:42.830
However, you frame it as
a test of sports intelligence,

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:47.150
all of a sudden that delta, flips.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:52.230
If you remind Asian women of their
Asianness before they take a math test,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.010
they'll do better.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.860
Remind them of their sex,
and they'll do worse.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.740
I hadn't heard about any of this
research before I started my first job

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:04.770
as a computer engineer.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.070
But if you had asked me what effect
I thought stereotypes around women in

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:15.230
engineering could have on me,
I would have said none.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.610
I graduated first in my class.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:22.975
I had several job offers and
I actually like nerd culture.

00:04:22.975 --> 00:04:26.685
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean I build robots for a hobby.

00:04:26.685 --> 00:04:30.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I tell dorky Star Wars jokes,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.315
even though I'm the only one
that ever laughs at them.

00:04:32.315 --> 00:04:34.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:39.350
even though I was in the minority on
my team, I felt welcomed from the first

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:44.360
day I started Until about
a year into my job.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:46.920
I hit a wall.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.650
It was an assignment I
just could not figure out.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.770
And with each failed attempt to crack it,

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.690
I felt a little more of
my confidence drain away.

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.770
Until suddenly,
I found myself thinking the unthinkable.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.250
Maybe I wasn't meant to be an engineer.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:14.320
I thought about quitting every day.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.820
I didn't realize it at the time, but that
right there, was the power of stereotype.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.950
It turned what should have been
a tiny setback into proof that

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.520
every stereotype I'd internalized
over my whole life was correct.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:30.988
I wasn't good enough.

00:05:30.988 --> 00:05:34.950
I didn't belong.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.510
I knew that I had to ask for help.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.100
And I knew it shouldn't be a big deal.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:45.590
And my team was super collaborative,
but I couldn't bring myself to do it.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:51.970
For me, asking for
help it meant becoming like Barbie.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.010
It meant admitting I had failed and

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:00.460
I wouldn't just be failing myself
I'd be failing women everywhere.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.680
As one of the few of us,
I stood for all of us.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.890
So I couldn't just be good,
I had to be exceptional so

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:15.490
that maybe I could disprove the stereotype
that women didn't belong in engineering.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:22.880
Try taking that weight on your
shoulders as a 22 year old.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.520
I've read research by Carnegie Mellon,
at Carnegie Mellon, with students there,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.310
and the experiences reported by
women were eerily similar to my own.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.220
Many came in as stellar math and
science students.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:40.650
But as they ran into challenges with
their introductory programming courses.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.520
They, too, began to believe
the voices they'd once rejected.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.870
Voices that told them they'd only been
admitted because they were women.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.030
It didn't matter that they
eventually mastered the material.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.100
They couldn't get it out of their heads.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:07:00.320
Of the women interviewed half
would drop out of the program, but

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.960
of those dropouts,
half were on the honor roll.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.780
These women aren't the only
ones paying the cost here.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:11.390
Businesses are missing out too.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:16.600
Companies that have more gender
diversity Have greater profits and

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:19.720
greater sales revenue
than their competitors.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.480
Studies show that teams that include
women are more innovative and

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.240
more productive than those that don't.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.370
So if we want to improve
our business outcomes,

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.540
keep women from dropping
out of these programs.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.330
Let's start by addressing the stereotypes.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:39.600
That are turning off not only women,
but anyone else who falls

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.260
outside of the narrow definition,
we what it means to be a Turkey.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.230
To other women out there,
please don't make the mistake of believing

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.570
you're the only one struggling
with these feelings.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:58.180
Ask for help when did,
get past what you are stuck on.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:03.010
And I guarantee one day you'll
be that person for someone else.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.940
So, those of you in the majority,
we need your help too.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.450
Understand that even
under the near confidence

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:18.060
a person that you could reach out to is
struggling against an invisible current.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.100
That they may not even know is there.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.460
Know that even your simple nudge of
encouragement can make all the difference,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:28.540
that's how I got past my wall.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:35.780
And finally, to everyone else,
all I ask is this.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:40.840
Be more mindful of the stereotypes
that you Unintentionally propagate.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:45.550
think about what it really means
when you tell someone, of course,

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:49.150
you're going to get into that program,
you're a woman, or

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:55.050
what you're really saying, when you tell
someone, you don't look like an engineer.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:01.240
The power of stereotype comes from
repetition over, and over again.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.450
Until the idea is ingrained
within our culture.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.670
Which means we're all guilty of
contributing to the problem.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.570
To quote from a fellow TED speaker,
the problem with our stereotypes around

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:23.580
computer scientists,it's not that they're
wrong, it's just that they're incomplete.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:32.445
There is space here for mathletes and
athletes, cake bakers and robot makers.

00:09:32.445 --> 00:09:34.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's start

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:39.920
by widening our definition of what
it means to be a computer scientist.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.330
So the next time you close your eyes,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:47.660
you can imagine Barbie coding
alongside Steven and Brian.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:55.991
And then together,
we can all build a future in which we can.

00:09:55.991 --> 00:09:57.878
Live long and prosper.

